#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "604fcbbb-6d34-5dca-8138-2dc99d524115" :question/math-academy-id "e-8875" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2$." :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a1aa679a-bbe1-5682-bca4-5103f5717be0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8876" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^2z-2y+z=6$.\nWhat is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}z - 2y + z &= 6 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 1)z &= 6 + 2y \\\\\nz &= (2(y + 3))/(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "d298fa26-3b8f-5561-944c-ae6c919867b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8877" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form\n\n$$\nz=f(r,\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} - 4z^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n4z^{2} &= r^{2} \\\\\nz^{2} &= \\frac{r^{2}}{4} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{r}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that\n\n$$\nz\\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nz= \\dfrac r2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black} \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black} \\dfrac r2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "d8feb600-a06a-581d-bd23-308c791d03e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8878" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨θ\\cos θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b9fd772-88bd-58a8-9b9f-0969f6743df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = vi + uj + (u^{3} - uv)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116495} {:answer/id #uuid "9094f663-7312-552f-83ba-167f3d3afaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = uvi + uj + vk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116496} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1caadb-9820-5e00-a6d6-983f45ec4c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = uvi + vj + uk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116497} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2d2445-6d3e-5b74-b8a5-72d476038351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = ui + vj + (u^{3} - uv)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498} {:answer/id #uuid "40f0b2dc-25e4-5e80-a854-3d43a95621d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = ui + (u^{3} - uv)j + vk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf2d2445-6d3e-5b74-b8a5-72d476038351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = ui + vj + (u^{3} - uv)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8315bf-dd83-58c0-bbc4-548ff228e244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116494}] :question/id #uuid "cced9a43-f637-5be6-a71f-17c0a9fd1fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103785" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = x^{3} - xy$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2f354e-eec2-5530-a10e-30a37d3893da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u^{3} + v^{2} - 2}" :db/id #edb/r